The (odoc <bool>) field of the package stanza controls whether the generated
opam file depends on odoc and builds the @doc alias.

  $ gen() {
  > cat > dune-project << EOF
  > (lang dune 3.22)
  > (using unreleased 0.1)
  > (generate_opam_files true)
  > $1
  > EOF
  > dune build foo.opam && cat foo.opam
  > }

By default, odoc is added to the dependencies and @doc is built:

  $ gen '(package (name foo) (allow_empty))'
  # This file is generated by dune, edit dune-project instead
  opam-version: "2.0"
  depends: [
    "dune" {>= "3.22"}
    "odoc" {with-doc}
  ]
  build: [
    ["dune" "subst"] {dev}
    [
      "dune"
      "build"
      "-p"
      name
      "-j"
      jobs
      "@install"
      "@runtest" {with-test}
      "@doc" {with-doc}
    ]
  ]
  x-maintenance-intent: ["(latest)"]

Explicitly enabling odoc is the same as the default:

  $ gen '(package (name foo) (allow_empty) (odoc true))'
  # This file is generated by dune, edit dune-project instead
  opam-version: "2.0"
  depends: [
    "dune" {>= "3.22"}
    "odoc" {with-doc}
  ]
  build: [
    ["dune" "subst"] {dev}
    [
      "dune"
      "build"
      "-p"
      name
      "-j"
      jobs
      "@install"
      "@runtest" {with-test}
      "@doc" {with-doc}
    ]
  ]
  x-maintenance-intent: ["(latest)"]

Disabling odoc removes both the dependency and the @doc target:

  $ gen '(package (name foo) (allow_empty) (odoc false))'
  # This file is generated by dune, edit dune-project instead
  opam-version: "2.0"
  depends: [
    "dune" {>= "3.22"}
  ]
  build: [
    ["dune" "subst"] {dev}
    ["dune" "build" "-p" name "-j" jobs "@install" "@runtest" {with-test}]
  ]
  x-maintenance-intent: ["(latest)"]

A user-written odoc dependency is kept as is:

  $ gen '(package (name foo) (allow_empty) (odoc false) (depends (odoc :with-test)))'
  # This file is generated by dune, edit dune-project instead
  opam-version: "2.0"
  depends: [
    "dune" {>= "3.22"}
    "odoc" {with-test}
  ]
  build: [
    ["dune" "subst"] {dev}
    ["dune" "build" "-p" name "-j" jobs "@install" "@runtest" {with-test}]
  ]
  x-maintenance-intent: ["(latest)"]

The documentation URL is still emitted:

  $ gen '(package (name foo) (allow_empty) (odoc false) (documentation https://example.com/doc))'
  # This file is generated by dune, edit dune-project instead
  opam-version: "2.0"
  doc: "https://example.com/doc"
  depends: [
    "dune" {>= "3.22"}
  ]
  build: [
    ["dune" "subst"] {dev}
    ["dune" "build" "-p" name "-j" jobs "@install" "@runtest" {with-test}]
  ]
  x-maintenance-intent: ["(latest)"]

Only the package that disables odoc is affected:

  $ gen '(package (name foo) (allow_empty) (odoc false)) (package (name bar) (allow_empty))'
  # This file is generated by dune, edit dune-project instead
  opam-version: "2.0"
  depends: [
    "dune" {>= "3.22"}
  ]
  build: [
    ["dune" "subst"] {dev}
    ["dune" "build" "-p" name "-j" jobs "@install" "@runtest" {with-test}]
  ]
  x-maintenance-intent: ["(latest)"]
  $ dune build bar.opam && cat bar.opam
  # This file is generated by dune, edit dune-project instead
  opam-version: "2.0"
  depends: [
    "dune" {>= "3.22"}
    "odoc" {with-doc}
  ]
  build: [
    ["dune" "subst"] {dev}
    [
      "dune"
      "build"
      "-p"
      name
      "-j"
      jobs
      "@install"
      "@runtest" {with-test}
      "@doc" {with-doc}
    ]
  ]
  x-maintenance-intent: ["(latest)"]

From lang 3.23, a package with a (dir ...) field gets its own build command.
The opam file is then generated next to the source one, as foo.opam.generated:

  $ mkdir -p dircase/foodir && cd dircase
  $ cat > dune-project << EOF
  > (lang dune 3.23)
  > (using unreleased 0.1)
  > (generate_opam_files true)
  > (package (name foo) (allow_empty) (dir foodir) (odoc false))
  > EOF
  $ dune build foo.opam && cat _build/default/foo.opam.generated
  # This file is generated by dune, edit dune-project instead
  opam-version: "2.0"
  depends: [
    "dune" {>= "3.23"}
  ]
  build: [
    ["dune" "subst"] {dev}
    ["dune" "build" "-p" name "-j" jobs "@runtest/foodir" {with-test}]
  ]
  x-maintenance-intent: ["(latest)"]
  $ cd ..

The field requires the unreleased extension:

  $ cat > dune-project << EOF
  > (lang dune 3.22)
  > (generate_opam_files true)
  > (package (name foo) (allow_empty) (odoc false))
  > EOF
  $ dune build foo.opam
  File "dune-project", line 3, characters 34-46:
  3 | (package (name foo) (allow_empty) (odoc false))
                                        ^^^^^^^^^^^^
  Error: 'odoc' is available only when unreleased is enabled in the
  dune-project or workspace file. You must enable it using (using unreleased
  0.1) in the file.
  Note however that unreleased is experimental and might change without notice
  in the future.
  [1]
